.TH SYSDTUI 1 2026-11-01 "sysdtui 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sysdtui \- edit systemd unit files with their documentation beside you
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sysdtui
.RB [ \-\-user ]
.RI [ unit " | " file ]
.br
.B sysdtui
.RB [ \-\-user ]
.B \-\-override
.I unit
.br
.B sysdtui
.RB [ \-\-user ]
.B \-\-new
.I name
.br
.B sysdtui \-\-lint
.IR file ...
.br
.B sysdtui
.RB [ \-\-user ]
.B \-\-list
.br
.B sysdtui \-\-dump\-catalog
.RI [ section ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sysdtui
is a terminal editor for systemd units.
It lists the units on the search path and opens one in an editor that shows
the focused directive's documentation, taken from the
.BR systemd.unit (5),
.BR systemd.service (5),
.BR systemd.exec (5)
and related pages, beside the field.
It checks the file as it is edited, can run
.B systemd\-analyze verify
on it, and after saving can reload systemd and restart or enable the unit.
.PP
Units owned by a package are edited through an override drop-in
.RI ( /etc/systemd/system/ unit .d/override.conf ),
as
.B systemctl edit
does.
Lines that are not changed are written back exactly, and the previous
version of a file is kept with a
.B ~
suffix.
When the target directory belongs to root,
.B sysdtui
installs the file with
.BR sudo (8)
and shows the commands before running them.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-user
Work on the user's units, as
.B systemctl \-\-user
does.
.TP
.BI \-\-override " unit"
Open the unit's override drop-in.
.TP
.BI \-\-new " name"
Start a new unit from a template.
A name without a suffix becomes a service; a path such as
.I /mnt/data
becomes a mount unit with the escaped name.
.TP
.B \-\-lint
Check the named unit files (or drop-ins, as
.IR unit .d/*.conf )
and print
.IR file : line : " severity" : " message"
for each problem.
Exits 1 if any error was found.
.TP
.B \-\-list
Print the units found with their state, origin and path.
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-catalog
Print the directive catalog as JSON, for all sections or those named.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version.
.SH KEYS
In the unit list:
.B enter
edit,
.B o
override drop-in,
.B F
edit a full copy,
.B n
new unit,
.B /
filter,
.BR t / T
filter by type,
.B u
switch between system and user units,
.B s
status,
.B l
journal,
.B r
refresh,
.B q
quit.
.PP
In the editor:
.B enter
edit a value,
.B space
cycle a boolean or enumerated value,
.B a
add a directive,
.B S
add a section,
.B d
delete,
.BR J / K
move a line,
.B n
next problem,
.B u
undo,
.B p
diff,
.B v
verify,
.B V
show or hide the verify output,
.BR [ / ]
scroll the documentation,
.B ctrl+s
save,
.B esc
back.
.PP
While editing a value:
.BR tab " or " down
next choice,
.BR shift+tab " or " up
previous choice,
.B enter
accept,
.B esc
cancel.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SYSDTUI_EMBEDDED_DOCS
When set, use the documentation embedded in the binary rather than the
installed man pages.
.TP
.B SYSDTUI_UNIT_PATH
A colon-separated list of directories that replaces the unit search path;
the first one receives new units and drop-ins.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/system
Where system units and drop-ins are written.
.TP
.I ~/.config/systemd/user
Where user units and drop-ins are written.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR systemd\-analyze (1),
.BR systemd.unit (5),
.BR systemd.service (5),
.BR systemd.exec (5)
